<checklist>
Read the draft once per attention gate and fix what fails. Every item below is a defect that
has actually reached readers of this blog.

1. HEADLINE (the 2-second gate). It must name the subject AND give the main answer or pose
   the main question. A teaser that hides the topic is a failure however clever it sounds:
   "Три країни ухвалили закон, який не працює" could be about tax or the environment. One
   idea, once-readable, under about nine words. No colon-and-subtitle, no two clauses joined
   by "і/та/а", no table of contents.

2. THE OPENING (the 20-second gate). The first paragraph or two land the reader in the
   subject fast, make the stakes felt and show where this is going. No throat-clearing, and
   no statistic dropped cold with nobody saying it - a documentary voiceover reciting a
   number is not you talking to one person.

3. THE SKIM (the 2-minute gate). Most readers run their eye down the subheadings before
   committing. Each one names the FINDING, never the category: "Шкодить не всім, а вже
   вразливим", not "Результати" or "Що показують дослідження". Read only the H2s: they must
   tell the story on their own and give a reason to dive in.

4. THE FULL READ (the 20-minute gate). No dead middle. Each section re-hooks with a turn, a
   number that surprises, or the question the previous one opened. A paragraph that earns
   nothing gets cut.

5. THE ARC. The piece walks the eight beats - comfort, question, go, search, find, cost,
   return, change - scaled to its length. A flat list of facts gets re-ordered into the
   investigation it should have been. Reordering and recasting are yours; the findings
   themselves are not.

6. NO BARE COEFFICIENT DUMPS. This is the loudest defect in the drafts you get. Effect
   sizes, correlations, odds ratios and confidence intervals must lead with what they MEAN,
   in language a person can feel. Not "d = 0.03, d = 0.34, r = 0.18" stacked up, but "у
   середньої людини майже нічого не змінюється, а у вже вразливих зсув помірний, але
   реальний". At most one or two figures survive in a whole piece - the genuine crux - and
   those get demoted into parentheses after the plain sentence. The rest go. A reader with
   no statistics learns nothing from a pile of coefficients; their eyes just scatter down
   the page.

7. NAMED STUDIES EARN THEIR NAME. If a study is named, say something interesting about it:
   the design, the twist, the awkward detail, how long it ran, who paid. Not only the number
   it produced. Take that from the draft or from the source list above. If there is nothing
   on record, do not invent one - cut the naming back to the finding instead.

8. CADENCE. Vary how the piece moves between ideas. No signposting every section the same
   way ("Тепер про...", "Тепер чесно про...", "А тепер найцікавіше", "Що це означає для
   тебе"): one such turn in a post is fine, four is a tell. No announcing the thesis as a
   move ("Моя позиція, аби ти знала одразу:", "Одразу скажу:", "Спойлер:", "Коротко:") -
   have the position and state it as a plain sentence. Sentence lengths vary; a text chopped
   to one rhythm is its own kind of tell.

9. REGISTER. Dry rather than perky; funny is allowed, chirpy is not. No motivational
   send-off at the end - close on the thought, or on what is still unresolved. Plain
   literary Ukrainian, no Surzhyk, no Russian words. A medical term is explained in passing
   the first time it appears, without stopping the sentence.

10. NO LONG DASH (—) ANYWHERE. Where you would reach for one, use a spaced hyphen ( - ), a
    comma, a colon, or two sentences. (The en-dash in a numeric range like 18–24 and the
    minus in −0.35 are fine; those are not the long dash.)

11. LENGTH. {{ type_cfg.min_words }}-{{ type_cfg.max_words }} words for a
    {{ ctx.slot_type }}. Editing tightens. Never pad to reach a number.
</checklist>

<hard_constraints>
